#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Place every order the REST API accepts, reading them from the Redis Stream `unified:orders:intents:stream`, and keep
placing until stopped.

    bin/unified/orders/order_engine
    redis-cli XINFO GROUPS unified:orders:intents:stream  # lag and pending count
    redis-cli GET unified:orders:engine:lock              # the pid of the engine that is running

This runs only when the REST API is configured with `UNIFIED_BROKER_INTERFACE_API_ORDER_PLACEMENT=engine`. In that mode
`POST /api/orders/place` checks the access token, validates the body and resolves the instrument, then writes the order
to the stream as field `intent` and blocks on `unified:orders:intents:result:<intent_id>` for the answer. This reads the
stream as the `engine` consumer group, places the order at the first broker the configured selector ranks that can take
it, and pushes the answer onto that key.

**Why the order engine exists.** An order placed from inside a gunicorn worker cannot outlive the request that asked
for it, so a bracket, an OCO pair or a trailing stop - anything whose behaviour is a reaction to a later fill, price or
clock tick - cannot be expressed. Everything reaching a broker through one process also makes a rate budget or a daily
loss lockout a property of the system rather than a convention repeated in each worker. This is the first stage of that:
it places plain orders only and behaves exactly as the REST API did, through a new path.

**It keeps a clock.** Its read of the streams blocks for about a second when nothing arrives, which is turned into a
tick rather than a thread, so the types waiting for a time of day - a nine-twenty entry, a half-past-two give-up, a
square-off before the broker's own - are woken without anything else touching a parent at the same moment. Only types
that ask for a tick are read at all, so an engine running nothing but plain orders does no extra work.

**It follows the orders it placed.** Besides the intents, it reads `unified:order-updates:stream` - the one stream
`bin/unified/orders/websocket_order_details` already fills with every broker's order updates - as a consumer group of
its own, and applies an update to the leg it belongs to. An update naming no leg of this engine's is acknowledged and
dropped, which is most of them. Both streams are read in one call, so a fill and a new order reach the same loop and
neither starves the other.

**Every order passes the same gates.** The daily loss lockout is checked before the order is even understood, so a
day that is past its limit costs nothing; it is off unless `UNIFIED_BROKER_INTERFACE_API_ORDER_DAILY_LOSS_LIMIT` is
set, and the engine says so at startup when it is not. The rate budget is a token bucket, global and per broker, taken
after the leg has been written down and before its request is sent, defaulting well under SEBI's ten orders a second;
a burst waits for a token rather than being refused, and only a burst outlasting the whole wait is refused. The
order-to-trade ratio is counted per broker and reported, and is what Stage 8's re-pricing types will consult. The daily
order count keeps each broker's placements for the day in `unified:orders:daily_count:<broker>`, expiring at 06:00 IST,
against the caps in `UNIFIED_BROKER_INTERFACE_API_ORDER_DAILY_CAPS` (`zerodha=3000,dhan=7000`); once a broker is within
`UNIFIED_BROKER_INTERFACE_API_ORDER_DAILY_CAP_EXIT_RESERVE` (a share, 0.05 by default) of its cap, new entries to it are
refused with 429 and only orders that close a position are sent, up to the cap itself. These are limits on placements: `PUT /api/orders/modify` and `DELETE /api/orders/cancel` still go straight from an API worker to
a broker and are not counted.

**What it does per order.** One or two Redis round trips and one broker call. The first reads the mapping marker and
every broker's login and settings; the second reads the instrument's catalogue entry and whatever the broker selector
queues, and is skipped when this process already holds the entry for the current warm and the selector queues nothing.
The instrument is not looked up here: the REST API resolves it and writes the id into the intent, so the rule that
decides an identity is unknown or ambiguous lives in one place.

**One engine at a time.** `unified:orders:engine:lock` holds this process's pid for 30 seconds and is refreshed every 10.
A second engine cannot take it and exits 1, because two engines reading the same group would place every order twice.
An engine that loses the lock to another process stops rather than placing orders beside it.

**An order that waited too long is not placed.** Each intent carries the deadline the API worker gave up at. An intent
read more than `UNIFIED_BROKER_INTERFACE_API_ORDER_ENGINE_STALE_INTENT_SECONDS` past that is answered 409 and recorded
rather than sent, so a restart cannot fire an abandoned order into a market that has moved.

**Nothing is placed twice on purpose.** An intent is acknowledged once its answer has been pushed, so an engine that
dies between placing and acknowledging redelivers the intent at its next start; that intent is almost always past its
deadline by then and is refused rather than placed.

**It rebuilds itself before it places anything.** Every transition is written to `unified.synthetic_order_events` and
committed before it is acted on, so on start the engine replays the day's rows through the same state machine the live
path uses and rebuilds every parent that had not finished. Legs are then brought up to date from each broker's own order
book. A leg still in `sending` means a crash between recording an order and hearing the answer, so the order may be live
at the broker under the caller's own tag: the orphan matcher attributes it only when exactly one unclaimed order matches
every field that was sent, within seconds of when it was sent, and otherwise parks the parent in `failed` for a person
to look at rather than guessing. Recovery reads Redis only for the brokers' order books, and rewrites its own keys from
what it found, so a flushed Redis costs a slower start rather than a lost position.

Exits 0 when stopped with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM after finishing the intent in hand; 1 when another engine holds the lock or
takes it while this one runs, when the event table cannot be prepared, or when its own state cannot be recovered; 2 for
a bad argument or configuration. Redis that cannot be reached while running is retried with backoff.
"""

import os
import sys
import socket
import signal
import argparse
import threading
from pathlib import Path

# Run under the project's virtual environment, and put the project on sys.path. See
# utilities/bootstrap.py for why the check is sys.prefix rather than the interpreter path.
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]))
from utilities.bootstrap import run_under_venv

PROJECT_ROOT = run_under_venv(__file__)

def main():
    """Place orders from unified:orders:intents:stream until stopped."""
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Place every order the REST API accepts, from unified:orders:intents:stream, until stopped.")
    parser.parse_args()

    from utilities.configurations import api_configuration, get_cache, get_logger, get_postgres
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.day_roll import DayRoll
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.engine_lock import EngineLock
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.engine_placement import EnginePlacement
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.clock_ticker import ClockTicker
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.daily_order_count import DailyOrderCount
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.engine_recovery import EngineRecovery
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.engine_runner import OrderEngine
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.order_update_follower import OrderUpdateFollower
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.loss_lockout import LossLockout
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.order_to_trade_ratio import OrderToTradeRatio
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.parent_store import ParentStore
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.price_ticker import PriceTicker
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.rate_budget import RateBudget
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.repricing_throttle import RepricingThrottle
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.risk_gates import RiskGates
    from unified_broker_interface.utilities.order_engine.utilities.synthetic_order_event_log import SyntheticOrderEventLog

    logger = get_logger("unified.order_engine")
    cache = get_cache()

    lock = EngineLock(cache, logger)
    if not lock.take():
        return 1

    event_log = SyntheticOrderEventLog(get_postgres, logger, f"{socket.gethostname()}:{os.getpid()}")
    try:
        event_log.apply_table()
    except Exception as exception:
        logger.error(f"Could not prepare unified.synthetic_order_events: {type(exception).__name__}: {exception}")
        lock.release()
        return 1

    try:
        placement = EnginePlacement(cache, logger)
    except ValueError as error:
        # A misspelt broker selector. Exit 2, which systemd does not restart, because retrying a
        # configuration that cannot work would hide the message in a crash loop.
        logger.error(f"The order engine is not configured correctly: {error}")
        lock.release()
        return 2

    placement.start_connection_warmers()

    parent_store = ParentStore(cache)
    recovery = EngineRecovery(cache, event_log, parent_store, placement.order_placement.broker_names, logger)
    try:
        recovery.recover()
    except Exception as exception:
        # Placing new orders while the engine does not know what it already has at a broker is the
        # one thing worse than not starting, so this is fatal rather than logged and carried on from.
        logger.error(f"Could not recover the engine's own state: {type(exception).__name__}: {exception}")
        lock.release()
        return 1

    stop = threading.Event()

    def _handle_signal(signal_number, frame):
        logger.info(f"Received signal {signal_number}. Finishing the order in hand, then stopping.")
        stop.set()

    for signal_number in (signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM):
        signal.signal(signal_number, _handle_signal)

    try:
        daily_caps = DailyOrderCount.read_caps(api_configuration["order_daily_caps"])
        daily_count = None
        if daily_caps:
            daily_count = DailyOrderCount(cache, daily_caps, api_configuration["order_daily_cap_exit_reserve"], logger)
    except ValueError as error:
        logger.error(f"The daily order caps are not configured correctly: {error}")
        lock.release()
        return 2

    gates = RiskGates(
        RateBudget(
            api_configuration["order_rate_per_second"],
            api_configuration["order_rate_per_broker_per_second"],
            api_configuration["order_rate_wait_seconds"],
            logger,
        ),
        LossLockout(cache, api_configuration["order_daily_loss_limit"], logger),
        OrderToTradeRatio(),
        RepricingThrottle(api_configuration["order_reprice_minimum_seconds"]),
        daily_count,
    )
    if daily_count is not None:
        logger.info(f"Capping each day's orders at {daily_caps}, keeping {daily_count.exit_reserve:.0%} of each cap for exits.")
    if gates.loss_lockout.is_configured():
        logger.info(f"Refusing new orders once the day is down {api_configuration['order_daily_loss_limit']}.")
    else:
        logger.warning("No daily loss limit is set, so nothing stops this engine placing orders on a losing day.")

    logger.info("Placing orders from unified:orders:intents:stream.")
    engine = OrderEngine(
        cache,
        placement,
        lock,
        logger,
        api_configuration["order_engine_stale_intent_seconds"],
        api_configuration["order_engine_result_ttl_seconds"],
        event_log,
        parent_store,
        OrderUpdateFollower(parent_store, event_log, logger, gates, placement),
        gates,
        ClockTicker(parent_store, event_log, placement, logger, gates),
        PriceTicker(cache, parent_store, event_log, placement, logger, gates),
        DayRoll(recovery, parent_store, logger),
    )
    exit_code = engine.run(stop)
    lock.release()
    return exit_code

if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
